---
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Annotation from '../components/Annotation.astro';
import PrincipleRefs from '../components/PrincipleRefs.astro';
import { localePath, t } from '../lib/i18n.js';

const locale = 'en';

const entries = (await getCollection('vendorQuestions', (e) => e.id.startsWith('en/'))).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.number - b.data.number,
);

const questions = await Promise.all(
  entries.map(async (entry) => {
    const { Content } = await render(entry);
    return { data: entry.data, Content };
  }),
);

const description =
  'Ten questions to ask any vendor whose product touches children, with the legal basis for each and a place to record the answer and the date.';
---

<BaseLayout title="Vendors" description={description} locale={locale}>
  <PageHeader
    caption="Vendors"
    title="Ten questions to ask any vendor"
    lede="A single-site program has essentially no negotiating leverage with a major vendor. What it can do is ask, record the answer with a date, and choose."
    locale={locale}
  />

  <p class="govuk-body">
    Nobody is going to redesign a product because one center asked. That is the honest
    starting point, and it is why this page is a list of questions rather than a list of
    criteria to score vendors against. Scoring assumes you can act on the score. Asking
    assumes only that you can write down what you were told, by whom, and on what date.
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    Three things follow from asking. You learn what the product actually does, which is
    often not what the sales page says. You create a dated record that protects you later,
    because "we asked in March and were told no" is a defensible position and "we assumed"
    is not. And when two products are in front of you, the answers are the comparison.
  </p>

  <div class="govuk-inset-text">
    <p class="govuk-body">
      The fourth thing follows from scale rather than from any one program. A field in
      which five thousand directors ask the same ten questions creates leverage that no
      individual director has. The questions below are deliberately identical for everyone,
      for that reason.
    </p>
  </div>

  <nav class="govuk-!-margin-bottom-8" aria-labelledby="contents-heading">
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-s" id="contents-heading">{t(locale, 'label.onThisPage')}</h2>
    <ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number">
      {questions.map((q) => (
        <li><a class="govuk-link" href={`#q${q.data.number}`}>{q.data.title}</a></li>
      ))}
      <li><a class="govuk-link" href="#recording">Recording the answers</a></li>
      <li><a class="govuk-link" href="#disclosure-card">The disclosure card, and eventually the register</a></li>
      <li><a class="govuk-link" href="#positive-pattern">A positive pattern worth naming</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="the-ten">The ten questions</h2>

  <ol class="govuk-list">
    {questions.map((q) => (
      <li class="govuk-!-margin-bottom-8" id={`q${q.data.number}`}>
        <h3 class="govuk-heading-m govuk-!-margin-bottom-4">
          <span class="govuk-caption-m">Question {q.data.number} &middot; {q.data.title}</span>
          {q.data.question}
        </h3>

        <Annotation label="Why it matters">
          <p class="govuk-body govuk-!-margin-bottom-0">{q.data.whyItMatters}</p>
        </Annotation>

        <details class="govuk-details" data-module="govuk-details">
          <summary class="govuk-details__summary">
            <span class="govuk-details__summary-text">What a real answer looks like</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="govuk-details__text">
            <q.Content />
          </div>
        </details>

        {q.data.authorities.length > 0 && (
          <Annotation label={t(locale, 'label.basis')}>
            <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet govuk-body-s govuk-!-margin-bottom-0">
              {q.data.authorities.map((a) => <li>{a}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </Annotation>
        )}

        <PrincipleRefs ids={q.data.governs} locale={locale} />
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <hr class="govuk-section-break govuk-section-break--l govuk-section-break--visible" />

  <h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="recording">Recording the answers</h2>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    The record is the whole point, and it is worth almost nothing in your memory a year
    from now. The Vendor Question Sheet sets out the ten questions with a place to write
    the answer, the name and role of the person who gave it, and the date it was given.
    Fill one in per product, keep it with your Approved Tools Register, and put the answer
    you were given next to the answer the other vendor gave.
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    <a class="govuk-link" href={localePath(locale, '/policies')}>Vendor Question Sheet and the rest of the policies to adopt</a>
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    Three habits make the sheet worth keeping. Date every answer, because an answer about
    model training or deletion describes an architecture that can change. Record who said
    it, because "their implementation lead" and "a chat agent" are different kinds of
    evidence. And keep the answer you did not like — a vendor that told you plainly that
    it trains on customer content has given you a more useful sheet than one that never
    replied.
  </p>

  <hr class="govuk-section-break govuk-section-break--l govuk-section-break--visible" />

  <h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="disclosure-card">The disclosure card, and eventually the register</h2>

  <div class="govuk-warning-text">
    <span class="govuk-warning-text__icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
    <strong class="govuk-warning-text__text">
      <span class="govuk-visually-hidden">Warning</span>
      This does not exist yet. It is phase three of the build sequence, and it requires
      adoption of the framework rather than agreement with it.
    </strong>
  </div>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    The proposal is small and specific: a standardized one-page vendor disclosure that
    answers these ten questions in a fixed format, published by the vendor rather than
    collected by you, plus a public register of the tools that have filed one. The model
    exists in healthcare and nowhere else. Directors in early childhood currently evaluate
    vendors with nothing.
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    It is achievable at a small scale. Twenty vendors covers most of the market in this
    sector, which is an unusually short list for an infrastructure project. Filing would be
    voluntary, and that is the only real incentive structure available here: a director
    comparing two products will notice which one did not file.
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    One unresolved question sits underneath it, and it is stated here rather than settled.
    A register implies that this site eventually takes a position on specific products, at
    least by omission. That is a meaningfully different institution — and a different legal
    posture — from one that publishes questions and lets directors ask them. Until that is
    decided, this page does the second thing.
  </p>

  <hr class="govuk-section-break govuk-section-break--l govuk-section-break--visible" />

  <h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="positive-pattern">A positive pattern worth naming</h2>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    Not every finding is a warning. Cognoa's Canvas Dx, the FDA-authorized autism
    diagnostic aid for ages 18 to 72 months, returns an indeterminate result in about 37%
    of cases by design, rather than forcing an answer it does not have. A tool that refuses
    to answer when it is uncertain is a pattern this site should hold up explicitly, and it
    is the only place on this site where a product is named.
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    An indeterminate result is not a failure of the tool. It is the tool declining to
    manufacture certainty about a child, and handing the question back to the people who
    can actually answer it. That is the behavior to ask about: what does this product do
    when it does not know, and can it say so?
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    The caveat belongs with the example. The authors of the same study could not analyze
    performance by race or socioeconomic status, because those data were not routinely
    collected. That is exactly the gap question 8 exists to close, in the one product held
    up here as a pattern worth copying.
  </p>

  <div class="govuk-inset-text">
    <p class="govuk-body-s govuk-!-margin-bottom-0">
      Before publication: the authorization status, the age range and the indeterminate
      rate above should be confirmed against the real-world performance study and the
      regulatory record rather than product materials, and the vendor should be contacted
      directly before being named on a public page.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
